        "slug": "the-system-of-ideas-for-what-for-who",
        "title": "The System of Ideas: For What, For Who, Message, Execution",
        "content": "### Chapter Outcome\n\nAt the end of this chapter, you should be able to turn customer evidence into a structured system of messages, concepts, hooks, and executions. You should know the difference between these layers and avoid treating a minor wording change as a completely new creative idea.\n\n## The System of Ideas: For What, For Who, Message, Execution\n\n### Why a System Matters\n\nRandom brainstorming creates random output. A System of Ideas gives the team a repeatable way to produce distinct concepts without losing the connection to real customer needs.\n\nUse this chain:\n\n> For What -> For Who -> Creative Message -> Execution\n\n| Layer | Meaning | Example question |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **For What** | The gap, tension, desire, or problem being addressed. | \"What progress does the user want?\" |\n| **For Who** | The audience context that makes this tension especially relevant. | \"Who feels this most strongly, and in what situation?\" |\n| **Creative Message** | The action, promise, reframe, or reason to care. | \"What should this person believe or do next?\" |\n| **Execution** | The visual/storytelling method used to express it. | \"How will we make the message felt and understood?\" |\n\nThe same problem can generate several messages. The same message can be expressed through multiple executions. This is how you build range without abandoning product truth.\n\n### Practical Exercise\n\nSelect one customer tension. Build a System of Ideas with:\n\n- Three different audience contexts;\n- Three messages for each audience;\n- Two distinct visual executions for each message.\n\nDo not write scripts yet. The goal is to generate a meaningful creative opportunity map.\n"
