        "slug": "reading-creative-feedback",
        "title": "Reading Creative Feedback",
        "content": "## Reading Creative Feedback\n\n### Feedback Is a Starting Point, Not a Verdict\n\nPerformance feedback should help the creative team form a better next hypothesis. It should not become a list of vague labels: \"this one is bad,\" \"make it more native,\" or \"the hook did not work.\"\n\nThe creator needs a basic distinction between business outcomes and diagnostic signals.\n\n| Signal category | Examples | Useful question |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Business / quality outcome** | Trial, paid conversion, revenue, downstream quality, retention signal. | Did the asset contribute to the type of user the business needs? |\n| **Attention signal** | Delivery, impressions, view / hold behaviour, CTR. | Did the asset earn an initial opportunity to communicate? |\n| **Expectation signal** | Clicks without downstream quality, comments showing confusion, mismatch between ad and destination. | Did the creative attract the right expectation? |\n| **Execution signal** | Comparative response across hooks, formats, talent, proof, or settings. | What execution pattern deserves a more disciplined test? |\n\nOne metric rarely tells the whole story. A high click response can mean strong relevance, misleading curiosity, or incomplete proof. A low-spending asset may still contain a valuable mechanism that needs a different execution. Ask questions before deciding.\n\n### Practical Exercise\n\nUse a real or hypothetical performance table. For each asset, write:\n\n1. One observation;\n2. Two plausible explanations;\n3. One next creative action;\n4. One thing you still do not know.\n"
