        "title": "Creative Learning Log and Taxonomy",
        "content": "## Creative Learning Log and Taxonomy\n\n### Institutional Memory Is a Competitive Advantage\n\nCreative teams lose time when they cannot remember what has already been tested, why a concept was retired, which hook worked for which message, or how a winner developed. A learning log turns isolated assets into cumulative knowledge.\n\n| Field | Example use |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Concept** | Group assets around the same customer/message territory. |\n| **Angle** | Record the strategic lens that made the product relevant. |\n| **Hook** | Track first-message and first-frame patterns. |\n| **Execution** | Track talent, setting, format, visual style, pacing, and proof. |\n| **Audience / market context** | Record the relevance hypothesis without overclaiming targeting certainty. |\n| **Outcome / signals** | Preserve key hard and soft signals from UA. |\n| **Decision** | Scale, iterate, retest, refresh, retire, or hold. |\n| **Learning** | One concise statement of what the team believes now and how confident it is. |\n\nA learning log is only useful if it is concise and consistently updated. Avoid writing paragraphs that no one can retrieve. Use a shared naming system and clear concept labels.\n\n### Practical Exercise (Chapter Assignment)\n\nCreate a **Creative Learning Log** with ten existing assets. Group them into concepts, identify their hooks and executions, and write one reusable learning for each concept.\n"
