        "content": "### Chapter Outcome\n\nAt the end of this chapter, you should understand that performance creative must protect user trust. The role is not to produce attention at any cost. It is to help the right user understand a truthful, relevant reason to consider the product.\n\nA creative that wins a click by creating fear, shame, unrealistic expectation, or false certainty may harm customer quality, platform standing, product trust, and long-term growth.\n\n## Product Truth and Credible Claims\n\n### What Product Truth Means\n\nProduct truth means the creative accurately represents what the product can do, how it works, what the user will receive, and what result can reasonably be expected. It does not require dull language. It requires that the promise and the product experience remain aligned.\n\n| Risky pattern | Better standard |\n| --- | --- |\n| Guaranteed result | Describe a supportable benefit, process, or intended outcome. |\n| \"Miracle\" transformation | Show realistic product use and accurate value. |\n| Claim that implies a diagnosis | Describe a general customer tension without claiming personal knowledge. |\n| Fear or shame as the main motivator | Name the problem respectfully and offer a constructive next step. |\n| Vague \"life-changing\" language | Explain what the product actually helps the user do. |\n\nWhen unsure about a claim, ask before production. The correct response is not to invent proof. It is to work with the product, legal/policy, or creative lead to find a precise and credible way to communicate the value.\n\n### Practical Exercise\n\nRewrite five risky marketing statements into product-truthful versions. For each rewrite, explain what changed and why the new version sets a more accurate expectation.\n"
